WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:07.350
- Welcome to StarTalk, I'm
your host Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.100
your personal astrophysicist.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.100
And I'm also the director

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.420
of New York City's Hayden Planetarium

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.400
at the American Museum of Natural History.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.290
And I got with me my co-host, Chuck Nice.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:17.123
- That's right.

00:00:17.123 --> 00:00:18.160
- Chuck Nice comic tweeting?

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.360
- Thank you sir, yes
that is indeed correct.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.401
- There you go.
- @ChuckNiceComic.

00:00:22.401 --> 00:00:23.480
Was there a Chuck Nice already taken?

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.800
Is that what happened?

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:25.913
- Yes, it was me.

00:00:25.913 --> 00:00:27.040
- (laughing) What did you...

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.730
- I actually didn't like
myself enough to cancel that,

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.080
and so I just started a whole new one.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.540
I'm starting all over
again, @ChuckNiceComic.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:35.770
- In case you didn't laugh at my tweet,

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.370
just remember that it's @ChuckNiceComic,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.210
that's how that works.
- Exactly.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.910
You're also a host of
a spin-off of StarTalk

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:45.803
that we're all quite proud
of, Playing With Science.

00:00:46.763 --> 00:00:50.350
'Cause what happened was
we had of our guests,

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.410
the portfolio of our guests,

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.840
the ones that were professional athletes

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.860
developed their own following basically.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.790
We figured let's spawn
that into its own show.

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.153
- I have spawned with
professional athletes.

00:01:01.153 --> 00:01:01.986
(Neil laughs)

00:01:01.986 --> 00:01:04.893
Never thought I'd be able
to say those words, yes.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.230
- And your co-host on that is?

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.100
- Gary O'Reilly who's a performer,

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.840
a former professional footballer.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.810
- Football area for the UK.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.330
- Crystal Palace and--
- Very cool.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.200
And today we're talking
about the evolution

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.620
of personal technology.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.770
- [Chuck] Wow.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.210
- Indeed.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.270
- That's a very rapid evolution.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:25.920
- Yes, it is, and I feature my interview

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.920
with YouTube vlogger
and all-around sensation

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.520
in this niche, we have Marques Brownlee,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.380
featuring in my interview.
- The kid?

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.944
Yes.
- That kid?

00:01:38.944 --> 00:01:39.777
- Yes.

00:01:39.777 --> 00:01:40.722
- Who does all the unboxing and stuff?

00:01:40.722 --> 00:01:41.880
- Yes.
- I love that kid.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.740
And he's also known online as what?

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.850
- MKBHD.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:48.655
- There you go.
- I love that kid.

00:01:48.655 --> 00:01:50.010
There you go.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.560
Featuring my interview with him.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.875
- Cool.

00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:53.708
- That was fun, just,

00:01:53.708 --> 00:01:56.170
'cause I feel like you okay,
go to bed now, it's past,

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.130
I felt like being his father.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.333
But no, he's got his own world,

00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:00.690
like create his own world.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:01.523
- He's big stuff.

00:02:01.523 --> 00:02:03.990
- Now since I only have
marginal expertise on this,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.470
generally we go out and
find the real expertise

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.570
as we did today, in studio we
have science and tech writer

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.900
for The New York Times and other outlets

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.600
including Wired, Clive Thompson, Clive.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:16.480
- Good to be here, my friend.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.313
- [Neil] Welcome.

00:02:17.313 --> 00:02:18.230
- It's great to be here.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.513
- Dude, dude, so you,

00:02:21.631 --> 00:02:23.370
you have a book from a few years ago.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.203
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:24.203 --> 00:02:26.170
- "Smarter Than You Think"?

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:27.003
- Yep.

00:02:27.003 --> 00:02:29.950
- "How Technology Is Changing
Our Minds For The Better.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:30.783
- Yes sir.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.520
- Wow, that is so counterintuitive.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.887
- You are the only one in the house.

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:37.390
- I'm the only one holding
down that argument.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.810
- Everybody else thinks
it's making us dumber.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.037
- If you need that book,
I have one for you.

00:02:42.037 --> 00:02:44.850
- All right, all right, none of us agree

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:48.130
with whatever you could
have possibly put in there.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:51.250
So in my interview with Marques Brownlee,

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:54.157
he runs a YouTube channel, MKBHD,

00:02:54.157 --> 00:02:56.000
it's a YouTube channel.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Five million subscribers,
700 million video views

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.740
and he reviews new tech and gadgets

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.350
that you might think of buying

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:07.670
or that you already bought

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.543
and then you learned you
shouldn't have. (laughs)

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.630
So any idea what makes it so popular?

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.440
'Cause I'm not a first adopter,

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.970
so I don't have to chase
the latest unwrapping.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.070
- But see that's why it's so popular.

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:22.903
- Why?

00:03:22.903 --> 00:03:24.810
- Is what you just said,
"I'm not a first adopter."

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.120
He is the first adopter in your stead.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.707
- Somebody has to be--

00:03:29.707 --> 00:03:30.540
- Somebody's gotta be the first.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.589
- The first adopter.

00:03:31.589 --> 00:03:32.422
- And that's what this kid does.

00:03:32.422 --> 00:03:33.303
- And also frankly I mean--

00:03:33.303 --> 00:03:35.300
- I gotta stop calling him a kid.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.515
He's a grown man, I'm just old.

00:03:37.515 --> 00:03:40.070
Okay, go ahead, sorry.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.810
- I think he's sort of an
exemplar of what's happening

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.820
at this new generation
of sort of broadcasters

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.529
are growing up not on TV but online.

00:03:48.529 --> 00:03:51.040
And they're really, I mean
he's really good at it.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.350
You look at it, it's
just beautifully shot,

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:55.440
he's just oozing charisma.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.170
- So in modern times one
perhaps shouldn't even make

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.000
the distinction between
being on TV or being online.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.040
- Yeah, in a weird way, I mean YouTube

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.410
is such a funny old phrase, right?

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:04.910
Because tube, I mean who actually

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:06.783
watching YouTube remembers--

00:04:06.783 --> 00:04:09.130
(Neil and Chuck talk over each other)

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:09.963
- [Neil] None of them.

00:04:09.963 --> 00:04:10.796
- None of them, I barely do.

00:04:10.796 --> 00:04:12.230
I've got a couple of
young kids in grade school

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.300
and middle school and for them,

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:16.803
what they watch, where they
learn things from is YouTube.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:21.240
Once we got a TV box hooked up to YouTube,

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.820
it's just YouTube on TV.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.350
There's no actual TV being watched.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:25.530
- Or they don't make the distinction.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:26.910
- That's right, absolutely true.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.680
- So that's an emergent fact.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.510
- I think it's beyond just
not making the distinction

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.063
'cause I have also an 11-year-old.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.950
He is resistant to television.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:44.990
The fact that he has
to do it on their time

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:47.070
and their schedule actually--

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.040
- And sit down in front of a
thing attached to the wall.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.590
- It upsets him, it's like how dare you?

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:52.640
- And there's also sort of I mean,

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.870
I think there's kind of
an authenticity that comes

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.100
or apparent authenticity,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.660
hard to say how authentic it really is.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.410
But from seeing someone
who looks maybe more

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.100
like someone you might
actually know doing this.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.400
Like my kids watch all
these Minecraft videos

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.113
and it's just someone doing Minecraft.

00:05:06.113 --> 00:05:07.870
Then a little box up in
the corner showing them

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:08.900
in their crappy little room--

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.617
- That's what my son does.
- There you go.

00:05:10.617 --> 00:05:12.160
It's how to play that game better.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.960
- That's right, yeah.
- Right, right.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.710
So the YouTube feels
more authentic and more,

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:19.360
like there's a real person there
than a very glossy TV show.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.470
- And they get like 12
million views. (laughs)

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.620
- On how to execute some
maneuver and that situation.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.740
So I sat down with Marques.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.840
So he's well known on the web of course

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:33.880
and I just asked him how
he got started on YouTube.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.210
So let's check it out.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:36.690
Apparently you've been doing this

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.860
since you were three years
old or something, is that--

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:41.510
- For a while, a number of years.

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.490
More than a majority of my life.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:45.590
- Most of your life, so
more than half your life.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:46.423
And how old are you now?

00:05:46.423 --> 00:05:47.256
- So I'm 22.

00:05:47.256 --> 00:05:48.440
- You getting old, yeah.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.750
I'll tell you how it happens later on.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.510
It's just downhill from 22.
- Yeah.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.630
So you're into gadgets.

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:57.930
- Yeah, into all kinds of tech.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:01.110
I got into cameras and I got
into all the tech around me,

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:03.600
and then this sort of
emerged into making videos

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:04.740
about the tech.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.823
- On a YouTube channel
that's a hugely popular place

00:06:07.823 --> 00:06:08.680
for people to go.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.100
- Right, and it didn't start that way.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:13.020
It started as just me making
videos for like myself

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.403
just to have that exist somewhere,

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:17.940
and then slowly people
started to discover it

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:19.853
and then it sort of grew from that.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.330
- So doesn't it cost money?

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:25.860
To review a gadget you
gotta own the gadget

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.300
or somehow obtain it.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.800
So what did you do?

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.740
- I was in school, so I was
using a laptop for school,

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.110
so I reviewed the laptop.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.840
And I reviewed a bunch of
free software on the laptop.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.810
And then I started viewing
paid things that I bought,

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.180
maybe the cooler for the laptop.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.990
That's the kind of stuff
that got you off the ground

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:46.250
and then you sort of proceed
to check out more elaborate

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:47.330
or extravagant things.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.900
- And then people come to depend on you.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:51.847
- A little bit, a little bit, yeah.

00:06:51.847 --> 00:06:53.850
- "I'm not buying it unless Marques--"

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.160
- When it's something you use as daily

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.570
as like a smartphone for example,

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:57.930
that's the kind of thing you do actually

00:06:57.930 --> 00:06:59.810
put a lot of research into and watch

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:02.300
a whole bunch of videos on
before you actually buy it.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.890
- So when did you realize you
started having a following?

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:06.910
- I think one of the turning
points is a video I did

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.780
about a web browser, when
Safari by Apple came to Windows.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:13.020
I made a video about that,
woke up the next morning

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.180
and I had a couple of
thousand views from people

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.470
who weren't subscribed.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:17.700
And that was kind of a light bulb moment

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:18.870
like oh, people actually kind of care

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.856
about this timely information.

00:07:20.856 --> 00:07:22.955
- And by then you had just
turned five years old. (laughs)

00:07:22.955 --> 00:07:25.430
- I think I was probably about
15 so I'll give myself that.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.083
- Same difference. (laughs)

00:07:28.083 --> 00:07:33.083
I just wondered, did your
parents worry about you?

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:36.211
If you're just playing
with gadgets, it's like,

00:07:36.211 --> 00:07:37.044
"He'll never amount to anything.

00:07:37.044 --> 00:07:38.469
"He's just playing with these toys."

00:07:38.469 --> 00:07:39.860
- I gotta guess yes.
(Neil laughs)

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.050
I don't actually know the
answer but I would guess yeah.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:45.870
- It's one of those I'm betting,
it's one of those things.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.720
He'll never amount,
they're shaking their head

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.760
as you're just playing
with your electronic toys.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.850
- Just editing a video, doing nothing.

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:56.430
- He's just doing nothing. (laughs)

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:00.045
Such is the, always the case
when the next generation

00:08:00.045 --> 00:08:02.940
defines what the future will be.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.270
So Clive, how would you distinguish
your childhood from his?

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.820
'Cause you started writing and you cared

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:11.040
about the same gadgets
and software that he did,

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:12.230
but not in that era.

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:13.590
- That's right, yeah, I mean this is like,

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.827
I grew up in the 80s, and
then when I got out of college

00:08:15.827 --> 00:08:18.720
in the 90s there's no smartphones yet,

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.590
there's barely an internet, right?

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:22.910
There was like a lot of these text BBSs--

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:24.500
- Al Gore had to invent it first, yeah.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.505
- Absolutely, message boards!

00:08:26.505 --> 00:08:27.710
- Message board, yeah, that's what it was.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:29.360
- Yeah, so I kind of had them and like,

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.960
so I could see the future
coming with message boards.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:33.930
I could sort of figure out
what was happening, right?

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.712
But yeah, my youth is very
very different and I think--

00:08:36.712 --> 00:08:38.630
- You would have come of age during

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.291
the earliest cellphones then, correct?

00:08:41.291 --> 00:08:42.870
Mid 90s, was it?

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:45.680
- No, no, I was well into my 20s

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:47.520
when the mobile phones came out basically.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.410
- Did you get the shoulder mounted one?

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:52.743
- Exactly, ones you can
use to deflect bullets.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:54.593
Yeah, I know.

00:08:55.518 --> 00:08:56.351
- Also calling Mars.

00:08:57.351 --> 00:08:59.670
- Exactly.
(Neil laughs)

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:02.262
I mean I think it was very
different and I think,

00:09:02.262 --> 00:09:03.680
I think they're some of the things

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:04.513
I would have liked about it.

00:09:04.513 --> 00:09:05.790
I mean I look at the online world now

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:07.050
and I think I wish I
had some of this stuff.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.670
I was in a band in high school, right?

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:12.810
So we make a couple of,
a small EP of music.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:13.643
Who heard that?

00:09:13.643 --> 00:09:15.912
No one, absolutely nobody.

00:09:15.912 --> 00:09:16.832
- So you wish you were
born 15 years later?

00:09:16.832 --> 00:09:18.434
Really what you're saying.

00:09:18.434 --> 00:09:19.267
- In some respect--

00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:21.240
- His band would have put
the thing out on YouTube

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:24.150
and ended up on Ellen, right?

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:24.983
- Ellen would have found it.

00:09:24.983 --> 00:09:26.850
- And before you know
it, it's just like whoa.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:31.650
- Okay, so I try to take a
cosmic view on these matters

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:36.283
and I just try to imagine in 20 years,

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:39.367
Marques Brownlee saying,

00:09:39.367 --> 00:09:41.860
"Gee, I wish I was born 15 years later."

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:42.970
'Cause there's something else

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:46.400
that would have made some next
generation even more potent.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:47.570
- YouTube of the mind.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:48.403
- Of the mind, yeah.

00:09:48.403 --> 00:09:50.340
We got a USB into the--

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:52.190
- You just jack in and
you close your eyes.

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:53.310
You don't even have to close your eyes.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:55.430
- And just sends it to
wherever it's, right, right.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:58.540
Implants in people, and we're
looking back in these days

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:03.230
as how archaic they were.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:07.020
So I'm just curious, in
terms of YouTube as a site,

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.300
are there other sites
now, as I understand it,

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:14.080
Facebook is now a huge
access point for videos.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:15.470
- I would say also Snapchat actually,

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:16.507
or Snap as they now call it.

00:10:16.507 --> 00:10:17.983
It's a really big deal.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:20.983
They started this channel,
it's kind of like,

00:10:20.983 --> 00:10:24.260
where people do like
semi-professional videos

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:27.240
but there's people who have
just kind of blown up on Snap,

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.092
I have trouble calling
it Snap, on Snapchat.

00:10:29.092 --> 00:10:33.390
That's a very very big video
area for young people now too.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:36.020
YouTube is still the gorilla in the room

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:37.590
but there are others.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:38.796
- So Snapchat.

00:10:38.796 --> 00:10:40.300
The thing about--
- Snap.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:42.270
Snap, right, crackle, pop,

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:44.850
that's what I know when I say Snap.

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:48.450
But Snap, the thing is that it seems

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:49.750
like all of their technology,

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:51.720
they're quite innovative

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:52.920
but it seems like all their technology

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.640
just gets stolen by these other sites.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:56.473
- That's right, yeah.

00:10:56.473 --> 00:10:57.390
- How are they going to stay in business?

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.140
- I don't know, 'cause Facebook basically,

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.070
Instagram essentially
copied everything they did

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:03.890
and now it turns out that
Instagram's blowing up

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.450
'cause people are doing that
kind of quick viral video thing

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.410
on Instagram, that's a good question.

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:09.653
I don't know.

00:11:09.653 --> 00:11:12.068
- Okay, okay, if you don't know who,

00:11:12.068 --> 00:11:14.023
give me the guy who does know.

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:16.203
- Wheel him in.

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:19.240
- So with phones coming out,

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:21.290
the next generation of
phones look pretty expensive,

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:23.410
the next iPhone is gonna be up there.

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.520
How influential do you
think those reviews are

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:27.180
in people's buying decisions?

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:28.040
- Well, I think they're awfully big.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.490
I mean when I talk to people and ask them

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:32.273
how they learn about things.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.790
I'll ask them and they're
like yeah, it's all YouTube,

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:36.920
all YouTube, they immediately go there

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:38.880
to try and figure out
what something looks like,

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:40.630
what it feels like, what it works like

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:42.260
and whether or not they
should spend money on it.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:45.110
So YouTube has become
the conduit for learning

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:46.700
about something before you buy it.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:47.533
- [Chuck] Absolutely.

00:11:47.533 --> 00:11:50.160
- So I asked Marques
to just sort of reflect

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.100
on how technology has evolved

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:57.410
over his short number
of years reviewing it,

00:11:57.410 --> 00:11:58.243
so let's check it out.

00:11:58.243 --> 00:12:00.660
So you're 22 now.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:04.063
So in your long career as reviewer,

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:10.390
what would you say changed most
since you were 15 let's say?

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:13.780
Or that has impressed you
most for what has changed.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:14.680
- I'll give two things.

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:17.640
One I talk about a lot
of handheld electronics

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:21.780
like smartphones,
tablets, laptops, displays

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.570
have impressed me a lot and--

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:25.740
- The quality and resolution, crispiness.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.620
- Detail, the crispiness,
all that stuff a lot

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:32.100
in the last couple of years
and also more recently cameras.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:33.890
Cameras in smartphones,

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:37.410
cameras in laptops, the
front-facing cameras

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.000
but especially in smartphones
have gotten a lot better.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.937
But the number one thing
I'd say is the displays.

00:12:41.937 --> 00:12:46.830
- The quality of displays
has enabled a whole industry

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:50.750
to land in that medium,
isn't that correct?

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:51.583
- Yeah.

00:12:51.583 --> 00:12:55.020
- It's not just making what
you saw previously better,

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:58.290
it is opening up entire new industries,

00:12:58.290 --> 00:12:59.180
is that a fair statement?

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:00.750
- Virtual reality is an example,

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:04.400
where you wouldn't have a
super high-resolution display

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.670
right up against your face
with a lens to focus on it

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:07.780
to put you in another world.

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:10.216
That would not be
possible with the displays

00:13:10.216 --> 00:13:11.912
four or five years ago.

00:13:11.912 --> 00:13:15.320
- Because that close to your face,

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:18.140
the lower resolution would
be unsellable to you.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:19.120
Is that a fair...

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:19.953
- [Marques] Yeah.

00:13:19.953 --> 00:13:22.936
- Okay, so we've crossed
certain thresholds

00:13:22.936 --> 00:13:26.550
of device innovation.

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:30.080
- Yeah, Apple might use
the term retina display

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:33.160
to describe a display where
the pixel density is so great

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:36.260
that your eye cannot
discern individual pixels.

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:38.700
Pretty much every phone has a
retina display at this point

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:41.597
where five years ago
that was not the case.

00:13:41.597 --> 00:13:43.820
And even the photos you
take with the cameras

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:46.333
getting so detailed and
such high resolution,

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:50.160
that again the type of images
you can take out of a camera

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:52.290
on your smartphone are
often better than something

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.450
you get from a dedicated camera
like seven, eight years ago.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:59.382
- Yeah, so Clive, can you
explain why this is happening?

00:13:59.382 --> 00:14:02.990
- Yeah, it's just demand
and supply, right?

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:06.537
- No, I didn't know the demand
of stuff that I'm buying now.

00:14:06.537 --> 00:14:08.700
It's like oh my gosh, I
guess it's kind of cool.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:10.617
- Once people start,
once the iPhone came out

00:14:10.617 --> 00:14:15.190
and people realized that they
want, 10 years ago, right.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:16.730
iPhone comes out and they don't want

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:17.600
a little flip phone anymore,

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.390
they want something that
has a screen on it, right?

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:22.190
So that produces an enormous
demand for those screens.

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:25.810
And over in Asia, the factories
start working and working

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:29.280
and working and working and
the price drops dramatically.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:32.390
And so the high end is always
chasing more and more pixels

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:34.500
and more and more resolution
and greater brightness.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.190
But even that has dropped and dropped

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:38.089
and dropped and dropped
to the point where like,

00:14:38.089 --> 00:14:40.000
it's an expensive part of the phone

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.150
but it's amazing how cheap you
can get really nice screens

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:44.983
now over in Asia.

00:14:44.983 --> 00:14:49.290
- So all right, now I have a
nice screen but like I said,

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:53.170
I never would have imagined
using my cell phone saying yeah,

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:55.000
I want to take a picture
with my cellphone,

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:56.293
this was not an urge.

00:14:57.356 --> 00:14:58.780
I don't think anybody had that urge.

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:00.900
- I think what might have driven that--

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:02.960
- I thought they were
forcing it on me initially.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:05.370
- Yeah, they did, they
were if you remember.

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:08.752
And I think what did it was
believe it or not, apps.

00:15:08.752 --> 00:15:11.980
The advent of apps and these photo apps

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:15.790
that allowed you to do your
own little like photoshopping

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.062
and to manipulate images

00:15:18.062 --> 00:15:19.573
and then post them.

00:15:20.572 --> 00:15:21.405
- Yeah, I mean I think in many ways

00:15:21.405 --> 00:15:22.910
a tipping point was
probably Instagram, right?

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:24.810
So that was the first thing

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:26.050
where you could take a picture

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:28.770
and you could put a filter on
it to make it look prettier

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:29.820
than what you did, right?

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:31.980
And this was an intentional
thing that Kevin Systrom,

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.750
the guy that invented it was talking about

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:36.130
with his girlfriend
when he was designing it

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:37.510
about sharing photos.

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:39.490
And she said to him, they
were talking about it

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:40.717
and she was like,

00:15:40.717 --> 00:15:41.550
"The problem is I don't
want to share my photos

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:42.383
"if they don't look as good as yours."

00:15:42.383 --> 00:15:45.447
He's a big analog camera guy and he said,

00:15:45.447 --> 00:15:47.587
"Well, that's because
I'm using all these films

00:15:47.587 --> 00:15:49.090
"that produce these
lovely filtering effect."

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:50.267
She said, "Well, you
need to put some filters

00:15:50.267 --> 00:15:51.100
"in that thing."

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:53.350
Lo and behold, that's exactly what worked.

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:55.680
Because you could take
pictures, make them look pretty,

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.170
instantly share them, that took off.

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:58.300
- Are we gonna go up against,

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:01.090
is there some Moore's law
of personal technology?

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:01.923
- [Clive] Yeah, there is.

00:16:01.923 --> 00:16:02.950
- And is that gonna end?

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:04.810
- Well, first of all guys, let me just say

00:16:04.810 --> 00:16:07.900
that there are some people
who may not be familiar

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:08.890
with what Moore's Law is.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:10.270
I mean I know what it is--

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:11.540
- Other people might not know.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:14.370
- Other people guess they many
not, but what is Moore's law?

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:17.174
- In brief Moore's Law is the observation

00:16:17.174 --> 00:16:19.420
several decades ago that computer chips

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:22.230
were getting basically twice
as fast every 18 months, right?

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:23.460
And that's because they could make

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:25.100
the components smaller
and smaller and smaller.

00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:26.950
They are now at the atomic level.

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:29.660
Those wires are so tiny you
can't make them any smaller.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:31.340
That is what's called
the end of Moore's Law.

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:33.020
So will the devices stop getting--

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:34.390
- And this is Gordon Moore?

00:16:34.390 --> 00:16:35.267
- Gordon Moore, that's right, yeah.

00:16:35.267 --> 00:16:38.200
- One of the founders of
Hewlett Packard I think,

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:39.410
is that right?

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:41.692
- Yeah, oh boy, you're
puttin' me on the spot here.

00:16:41.692 --> 00:16:43.388
Either that or--
- Mr. Tech Journalist.

00:16:43.388 --> 00:16:47.380
Or Fairchild Semiconductor,
I'm blacking out.

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:48.540
- But you know the issue.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:52.130
When the wires are so small and
they reach atomic distances,

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:55.380
then quantum effects between
wires start taking over.

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:56.680
- Sweet.
- It is sweet.

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:58.030
That is awesome.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:02.220
- So the wire, no longer
know that it's independent

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:03.380
of the wires sitting next to it.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:04.213
- Absolutely.

00:17:04.213 --> 00:17:07.110
- Because the wave function
talks to them both.

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:08.903
- Dude, that's amazing, I love it.

00:17:09.926 --> 00:17:11.990
- So yeah, so they're
essentially having to figure out

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:15.290
how to make things faster
without making them smaller.

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.653
- Okay, so fine, so even if you
do level off at Moore's Law,

00:17:21.016 --> 00:17:23.970
but presumably design can change.

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:25.320
Are there other limits?

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:28.960
Price, cultural, design?

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:30.940
- At the moment actually
weirdly one of the big limits

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:33.130
is how fast can you
get data to that device

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:35.300
from like a cell phone tower, you know?

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:37.050
That's almost a bigger limit now

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:38.320
than the speed of the phone.

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:40.330
- It's like cable TV,
you Know what I mean?

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:42.130
- They talk about how great your cable is,

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:44.190
we have this fiber optic and
whatever, that's like yeah,

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.993
I still got copper running into
my house, who gives a damn?

00:17:46.993 --> 00:17:49.840
I actually fell for that
crap and I was just like

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:52.330
this is the exact same cable I had before.

00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:56.347
- So can we think of these
smartphones as augmenting

00:17:57.760 --> 00:17:59.760
our human physiology?

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.870
- Absolutely, sure, I mean
every technology we've had

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:05.050
has to a certain extent
as Marshall McLuhan put it

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:07.570
been an extension of man, right?

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:10.510
So TV extended our vision,

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:12.870
the phone extended our
ability to speak and talk.

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:17.150
These things are the strongest
extensions of humanity

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:18.010
that we've ever had.

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:20.260
And I would say that
you could think of them

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:22.520
as almost like a sense apparatus.

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:25.910
They're like a form of
ESP by which we tap into--

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:28.183
- Not ESPN.
- Not ESPN exactly.

00:18:28.183 --> 00:18:29.879
(Chuck vocalizes)

00:18:29.879 --> 00:18:32.010
- Okay, but a form of ESP.

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.080
- Yeah, you could of 'em as a sense of ESP

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:36.300
by which we sense what
other people are thinking

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:38.300
and talking about and
doing like all the time,

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:39.500
for good and for ill, right?

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:40.620
Because on the one hand, wow,

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:41.800
you learn a lot of great things.

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:43.790
On the other hand it's
almost like having telepathy

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:45.100
and being able to shut,

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:48.139
being unable to shut other
people's voices off in your head.

00:18:48.139 --> 00:18:50.970
- In fact, when I tweet and I
look at the responses to that,

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:54.880
I usually tweet something
educational, it's my intent.

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:57.518
And I try to add a little
funny in it if I can,

00:18:57.518 --> 00:18:58.351
do a little--

00:18:58.351 --> 00:18:59.937
- You do a good job, I'm gonna say.

00:18:59.937 --> 00:19:04.090
(Neil and Chuck talk over each other)

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:07.710
So I look at the response
to it and that informs,

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:10.920
I see that as a neurosynaptic snapshot

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:14.440
of the instantaneous
reaction to words I use,

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:17.290
to phrases I compose, to
ideas I put out there.

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:19.610
And then when I give a
public talk, I fold those in.

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:21.300
And I'm a way better
communicator because of it.

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.149
So that's my ESP in a sense,

00:19:23.149 --> 00:19:26.640
getting inside the head
of a million people.

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.214
- I just figured out, I'm
doing Twitter all wrong.

00:19:29.214 --> 00:19:31.870
(everyone laughs)

00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:35.620
- Coming up more of my
interview with Marques

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:41.420
and it's an all, in
his capacity to tell us

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:43.970
what's going down in
the technology spectrum

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:45.333
when StarTalk returns.

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:49.650
We're back on StarTalk,
I'm Neil deGrasse Tyson.

00:19:49.650 --> 00:19:53.660
Co-host Chuck Nice, in studio
guest today, Clive Thompson,

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:55.870
a tech writer for the
New York Times and Wired

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:59.885
and other venues I presume--

00:19:59.885 --> 00:20:00.718
- A few others.

00:20:00.718 --> 00:20:01.832
- A few others, a few others.

00:20:01.832 --> 00:20:03.530
We feature my interview
with Marques Brownlee.

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:08.530
He's a 20-something, early
20s reviewer of tech devices

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:11.360
that you might think of getting.

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.720
He does this on the internet,
has millions of followers.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:16.163
And so I got here, sort of,

00:20:19.286 --> 00:20:22.430
a interesting sort of perspective, okay?

00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:27.430
On what role this portable
technologies might play today.

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:30.670
So I just had to ask him
what that relationship was

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:32.950
between these technologies and our lives,

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:33.783
let's check it out.

00:20:33.783 --> 00:20:36.160
You've only really ever known the internet

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:39.080
as a thing in your life rather than having

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:42.240
to have transitioned from
walking to the library

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:44.300
to get information and data.

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:48.250
So to you, your life
is practically defined,

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:52.920
enabled by information
you get on the internet,

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:56.190
what those apps do for you

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:57.503
as you conduct your life.

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:00.550
So there's a certain amount of trust

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:02.000
that you place in this information,

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:03.820
in the integrity of this information.

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:06.180
- I have an app on my
phone called Google Now,

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:08.540
and if I go to class enough times

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:09.490
with my phone in my pocket,

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:11.260
it knows that I go there every day.

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:13.010
So it puts that down as where I work

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:15.257
but it knows I go there every day.

00:21:15.257 --> 00:21:18.230
And if it's far enough away
that I drive there every day,

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:20.660
it'll start to show me
a card in the morning

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:25.600
of when I should leave based
on traffic to get there on time

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:27.220
because I go there the
same time every day.

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:29.030
So now it's helping me get there on time

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:31.193
because it knows where I go every day.

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:34.255
If there's an accident,
- So it's watching you.

00:21:34.255 --> 00:21:35.770
it'll wake me up earlier to tell me

00:21:35.770 --> 00:21:37.310
you should head out earlier.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:39.361
- Because there's an
accident on the road there.

00:21:39.361 --> 00:21:40.470
- There's an accident on the
road you're about to take

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:42.160
to get to the place you're about to go.

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:43.449
- And you're okay with all this?

00:21:43.449 --> 00:21:45.949
- I'm okay with all of it
because it's helping me.

00:21:47.090 --> 00:21:48.420
People don't like to think

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:50.070
that Google knows all this about us,

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:51.670
they're going to use this information

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:54.220
or even machines that are
collecting the information

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:55.053
can turn and use it.

00:21:55.053 --> 00:21:58.430
I feel like if they're
giving back this information

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:00.928
to help me in this way,
that's actually really useful,

00:22:00.928 --> 00:22:02.230
I'm okay with it.

00:22:02.230 --> 00:22:04.140
- So I'm the senior citizen
among the three of us.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:06.950
In my day, privacy was paramount.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:07.783
- Absolutely.

00:22:07.783 --> 00:22:10.290
- And I don't know that
this next generation

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:12.030
gives a rat's ass about privacy.

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:13.186
- Not at all.

00:22:13.186 --> 00:22:14.185
- All their stuff is on the internet.

00:22:14.185 --> 00:22:17.300
Their height, their weight,
their sexual preference,

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:21.890
their gender pronoun
and can you just offer

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:25.300
some reflections on the meaning of privacy

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:27.923
in the era of quote, helpful.

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:31.486
- It's definitely
changed a lot for exactly

00:22:31.486 --> 00:22:33.530
the reasons we've been talking about.

00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:35.470
It is sort of impossible
if you're young right now

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:38.240
to have a social life
without participating

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:40.182
in all these apps.

00:22:40.182 --> 00:22:41.170
- [Neil] Social apps.

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:42.010
- Social apps, exactly.

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:44.388
Like whether that's Snapchat
or Facebook or whatever,

00:22:44.388 --> 00:22:47.263
you're kind of off the grid.

00:22:47.263 --> 00:22:48.700
And there are abstainers,
it's kind of funny.

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:50.260
You will find young people who are like,

00:22:50.260 --> 00:22:52.690
I will have no truck with this.

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:54.980
But they're the ones who are
like kind of that iconic class

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:56.430
who don't really care about,

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:59.260
about having a big group of friends.

00:22:59.260 --> 00:23:01.550
So I think one of the
problems young people have

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:02.870
is that they're,

00:23:02.870 --> 00:23:04.320
it's not even necessarily that they have

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:06.960
a fundamentally different
attitude towards privacy

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:08.900
but they have no choice but
to do these types of things

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:11.760
in the same way that maybe
for us when we were younger,

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:13.230
not getting a driver's license

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:16.450
was sort of basically a vow
of I'm never going anywhere

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:17.790
that the cool kids are going, right?

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:19.270
You could have said--

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:20.290
- I grew up in New York City.

00:23:20.290 --> 00:23:22.210
- Yeah, okay, I grew
up in Suburban Toronto

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:23.300
so you needed to drive.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:27.410
So back then, if you opted
out from that technology,

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:29.590
you had a real social trouble, right?

00:23:29.590 --> 00:23:32.350
- So these are cultural
social forces operating.

00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:33.710
And if you're in the middle of it,

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:36.170
you don't even know that
there was another option.

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:38.470
- Yeah, but my problem is that there is,

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:40.680
you heard in his statement was resident

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:42.930
a certain amount of trust
in the responsibility

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:43.840
that Google has for--

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:45.840
- Yeah, yeah, he's
trusting these companies.

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:47.954
- [Chuck] He was very trusting.

00:23:47.954 --> 00:23:49.830
- What's your trust level?

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:51.191
- Wow.

00:23:51.191 --> 00:23:53.456
- I don't trust anybody.

00:23:53.456 --> 00:23:57.110
Not in a conspiracy way,
it's just I don't want you

00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:58.130
knowing this about me.

00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:00.034
- Because it's power and
we all know power corrupts,

00:24:00.034 --> 00:24:01.416
that's what I'm saying.

00:24:01.416 --> 00:24:02.966
- Knowledge about you is power.

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:06.530
- Franklin Foer--

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:09.248
- We sound like old people
on the couch. (laughs)

00:24:09.248 --> 00:24:10.081
- I was about to say,
lemme tell you something.

00:24:10.081 --> 00:24:13.470
The only way this could have
got worse, here's Google,

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:14.870
okay, especially the black community,

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:18.660
here's Google if it had
been invented 25 years ago,

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:20.437
Google and the black community.

00:24:20.437 --> 00:24:22.554
"Why don't you mind your damn business?"

00:24:22.554 --> 00:24:25.760
(everyone laughs)

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:29.839
That's basically, that's
the response for, go ahead.

00:24:29.839 --> 00:24:31.980
- Franklin Foer came up with a book,

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:33.300
he talks about big tech, right?

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:35.033
And he means the great big huge companies

00:24:35.033 --> 00:24:37.210
that have massive amounts of power;

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:40.430
Facebook, Apple, Microsoft and Google.

00:24:40.430 --> 00:24:42.210
And he makes a distinction between that

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:44.300
and like kind of smaller
tech which are companies

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:45.850
you might actually know and trust.

00:24:45.850 --> 00:24:46.683
And I think that's a good distinction

00:24:46.683 --> 00:24:48.170
because what you guys--

00:24:48.170 --> 00:24:49.490
- No, but all those big
ones used to be little.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:50.440
Don't tell me that.

00:24:51.358 --> 00:24:52.210
They're just big now.

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:53.230
- I agree but like,

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:54.971
so the problem we have--

00:24:54.971 --> 00:24:55.890
- [Neil] They weren't born big.

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:57.840
- But they're small tech,
you can completely trust,

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.290
like open source stuff like
the Tor Project, right?

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:02.820
So their whole point is
to try and make it easier

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.120
that you'd have privacy
online and it's open source

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:09.030
and it's like a run for
nonprofit, civic minded hackers.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.470
There is technology out
there that you can trust,

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:14.900
it's not the stuff that is
big and is rewarded heavily

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:16.616
by the market.

00:25:16.616 --> 00:25:18.920
(everyone talks over each other)

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:20.733
- It's all about the commercial entities.

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:24.150
- Is there anything out
there that you've said

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:26.500
yeah all right, that crosses the line?

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:27.820
- Oh yeah, sure.

00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:29.690
I'll give you an example,
like so there was,

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:32.090
I came home from a summer vacation

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:35.730
and I opened up my Android
phone and Google said

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:38.370
hey we just put together a slideshow

00:25:39.614 --> 00:25:42.890
of photos and like points on
a map of all that I'd done

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:44.887
the last two weeks just 'cause
we thought you might like it.

00:25:44.887 --> 00:25:46.520
And it was so creepy, right?

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:47.490
It was super creepy.

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:50.750
- And I always turn my
locations off, okay?

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:52.210
But sometimes you forget

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.460
and I got that same doggone slide show

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:56.520
and it freaked me out.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.070
- It doesn't matter, I'll tell you why.

00:25:58.070 --> 00:25:58.903
- Why?

00:25:58.903 --> 00:25:59.810
- I'll tell you why.

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:01.520
As I understand it, okay?

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:05.810
Can't Google now, if you put
your picture up in the cloud,

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:10.810
it can do an image recognition
of your surroundings,

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:12.710
compare it with all their satellite feeds

00:26:12.710 --> 00:26:16.280
and say was this in the
Plaza de Piazza of the duh

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:18.720
and shall we ID it that way?

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:20.620
- Absolutely, in fact actually if you,

00:26:21.546 --> 00:26:23.730
if you ask me for the technology right now

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:25.640
that most unsettles me,

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:27.430
it's the galloping roll out

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:30.140
of face recognition
technology into everything.

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:33.071
And so I'm like I don't want my microwave

00:26:33.071 --> 00:26:35.290
recognizing my face.

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:37.690
I don't want omnipresence face recognition

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:41.230
all over the place, but
it's getting so easy to do.

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:43.450
I mean just the other
day Google helpfully said

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:45.360
hey guys, we just made TensorFlow,

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:47.150
our AI framework so it can run on

00:26:47.150 --> 00:26:49.312
like basically a Raspberry Pi.

00:26:49.312 --> 00:26:51.680
- Yeah, that's crazy,
that's insane by the way.

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:53.840
And let me tell you something else,

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:56.743
here's what really scares me
about the facial recognition.

00:26:57.750 --> 00:26:59.380
aside from the fact that
I don't, like you said,

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:01.840
I don't want my microwave
recognizing my face

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:06.260
is the fact that once somebody
learns how to hack that

00:27:06.260 --> 00:27:08.981
and make my face, like "Face Off" okay?

00:27:08.981 --> 00:27:11.630
Now they could just walk around and be me.

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:14.640
- However, as I understand
it, it's not just,

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:17.230
it's better than what you would think,

00:27:17.230 --> 00:27:19.140
it's better than how you would do

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:21.080
thinking that you're looking at yourself.

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:23.950
Because it's actually
measuring ratios of dimensions

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:26.340
of your face that are
extremely hard to duplicate.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:27.760
- Oh no, I'm not talking about the,

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:32.050
I'm talking about going beyond
the actual facial recognition

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:37.050
where that I go in and
tell the software that,

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:40.340
so I'm Neil deGrasse Tyson.
- You hack it.

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:43.440
I hack and say, so now
everywhere Neil deGrasse,

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:44.850
everywhere I go,
- You're Neil deGrasse.

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:46.223
I'm Neil deGrasse Tyson.

00:27:47.139 --> 00:27:50.030
That's what scares me,
and it's going to happen.

00:27:50.030 --> 00:27:53.570
- This brings us to, because
you can't outprogram it,

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:55.900
you can just hack it in the backend.

00:27:55.900 --> 00:27:56.733
- [Chuck] Always go around.

00:27:56.733 --> 00:27:57.960
- So this brings me to the concept

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:00.430
of internet identity, okay?

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:02.070
Who are you on the internet and how,

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:04.754
and privacy breaches, this sort of thing.

00:28:04.754 --> 00:28:09.754
So I had to ask him,
Marques Brownlee just,

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:16.983
is the internet not only
helping us but now defining us?

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:21.740
Defining our identities
and what it kind of do,

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:22.740
let's check out the interview.

00:28:22.740 --> 00:28:27.740
So is your life being defined
by all the ways the internet

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:31.913
inserts its way into your routine?

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:36.200
Because in my generation, it's still this,

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:39.150
it's a luxury, it's a
convenience, it's a tool.

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:41.850
It is not me.
- Right.

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:43.230
Has it become you?

00:28:43.230 --> 00:28:45.840
- So I would say yes, the
internet is part of our life

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:49.720
and always has been in a way
that other generations exist

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:51.770
alongside the internet and may go over

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:53.520
and use it once in a while.

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:57.980
I feel like everything we do
now whether it's communication

00:28:57.980 --> 00:29:00.010
or sharing anything with anyone,

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:02.370
whether it's someone I know
or just posting something

00:29:02.370 --> 00:29:06.340
for the world, all of it
goes through the internet.

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:07.670
The convenience stuff like I said

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:10.870
where a normal going to work routine

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:13.883
would have no internet
involved in it in 1990,

00:29:14.890 --> 00:29:17.440
today it's relying upon Google Maps

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:19.770
and my alarm clock on
my phone and everything

00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:21.310
telling me when to wake
up and when to leave

00:29:21.310 --> 00:29:22.363
and how to get there.

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:27.050
So in that way I do think
we are completely tuned

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:29.560
into the internet in a
way that's not the same

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:31.140
as any other generation, yeah.

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:33.370
- But can I say, it's not
that so much you are tuned

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:36.130
into the internet, the
internet's tuned into you.

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:37.090
- That also is true.

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:40.420
- Do you have an identity outside
of how you are represented

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:41.486
on the internet?

00:29:41.486 --> 00:29:43.310
You've got a Facebook
page, this is what I am,

00:29:43.310 --> 00:29:45.650
this is how I want the world to see.

00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:47.680
- It's almost like a pair of identities

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:49.450
where you have your social life

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:53.300
but you have your social
media life as well.

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:54.470
And sometimes they're different.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:56.060
You'd be surprised how different they are

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:58.860
with a single person having an online life

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:01.420
versus an offline life
which is kind of weird.

00:30:01.420 --> 00:30:02.970
- Okay, how close are the two for you?

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:04.180
- For me I'm the same person.

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:06.399
- We'll be the judge of that. (laughs)

00:30:06.399 --> 00:30:07.578
- You can look it up.

00:30:07.578 --> 00:30:09.660
I'm out there, it's all the same.

00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:14.610
- So I wonder, if and one
of my favorite comics from

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:17.317
was the 1990s New Yorker.

00:30:17.317 --> 00:30:20.520
There's a dog at the computer.

00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:21.760
You know the one I'm talking about.

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:24.657
And when he says to the dog next to him,

00:30:24.657 --> 00:30:25.490
"Good thing about the internet,

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:27.963
"no one knows you're a dog." (laughs)

00:30:27.963 --> 00:30:29.393
- That's pretty awesome, yeah.

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:33.930
- So what does it mean and how real is it

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:36.130
for people to just have a dual identity?

00:30:36.130 --> 00:30:39.260
The internet identity and
then their actual selves.

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:40.820
- I'll bite that off first.

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:44.350
I mean we've always had
multiple identities.

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:47.810
In fact in the early 20th century,

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:50.240
and late 19th century, we
had a migration of people

00:30:50.240 --> 00:30:51.370
from small towns to cities.

00:30:51.370 --> 00:30:54.220
And one of the things I loved
about it was in the small town

00:30:54.220 --> 00:30:57.350
everyone knew your business
and it was sort of impossible

00:30:57.350 --> 00:30:59.760
to reinvent yourself or to
discover other sides of yourself

00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:01.160
because everyone's like no,
that's not who you really are,

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:02.870
I know who you are Clive Thompson, right?

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:04.840
You go to the big city and
suddenly it's like lo and behold

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:06.670
wow, I could be someone different

00:31:06.670 --> 00:31:07.980
or I could be a couple
of different people.

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:09.460
I'm one person at work and then I go off

00:31:09.460 --> 00:31:10.555
and I do my thing with my crab.

00:31:10.555 --> 00:31:12.662
- Just a quick thing,
there are people who say,

00:31:12.662 --> 00:31:14.720
the city is so crowded,

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:17.750
I want to go out into
a rural suburban area

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:20.010
where no one will know me.

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:21.120
It's the opposite. (laughs)

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:23.190
- They will figure you out in 15 minutes.

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:26.930
- Yes, yes, whereas in the
city, no one gives, cares.

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:27.870
There's too many people to care.

00:31:27.870 --> 00:31:29.370
- To know you.

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:31.490
- So I think having multiple
identities is actually healthy

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:34.390
and a good thing frankly.

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:36.100
- [Neil] You just said, repeat that.

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:38.030
- I think having multiple identities,

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:40.310
as long as they're not sociopathic

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:41.940
and you have like a different postal box

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:44.900
where you sort of order--
- Damn!

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:46.157
- (laughs) Chuck!

00:31:46.157 --> 00:31:48.613
- For a moment I thought
I was getting approval.

00:31:50.530 --> 00:31:54.270
- Anyway, that's healthy and
there's an extent to which

00:31:54.270 --> 00:31:58.670
I think you see people
trying to do that online

00:31:58.670 --> 00:32:00.960
and kind of failing
because large corporations

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:01.980
don't want you to have that.

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:03.110
They want to know everything about you,

00:32:03.110 --> 00:32:04.330
they want to assemble it all in one place

00:32:04.330 --> 00:32:05.830
so they can target ads at you.

00:32:06.902 --> 00:32:10.450
This is Mark Zuckerberg's
original statement of Facebook,

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:14.390
having different selves is
a sign of inauthenticity.

00:32:14.390 --> 00:32:15.750
And of course he's gonna say that

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:17.060
because he wants to have
everything about you

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:18.390
so he can target ads at you.

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:23.250
- And where he's, I was about
to say failing miserably

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:25.980
but then I thought this is who I am

00:32:25.980 --> 00:32:28.350
and I'm saying that he's failing,

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:29.686
so that doesn't make sense.

00:32:29.686 --> 00:32:33.490
But I think what he's not
taking into account is the fact

00:32:33.490 --> 00:32:37.780
that when you do have
your different selves,

00:32:37.780 --> 00:32:39.580
they can also be authentic.

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:40.413
- Sure, absolutely.

00:32:40.413 --> 00:32:43.250
- You can have different selves
that are authentic selves.

00:32:43.250 --> 00:32:44.190
- That's my point.

00:32:44.190 --> 00:32:46.570
So I play in bands, I'm a journalist.

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:48.760
Often those two crowds have no idea

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:50.346
about the other part of me, right?

00:32:50.346 --> 00:32:51.760
Like when I go and hang with musicians,

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:53.060
some of them don't read the stuff I do.

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:55.090
They're just that's Clive,
the guitar player, you know?

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:56.260
- [Chuck] Exactly.

00:32:56.260 --> 00:33:00.070
- So ultimately are
there legal ramifications

00:33:00.070 --> 00:33:02.060
to access to your private information?

00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:04.282
- Well, I mean that's a good
question, I'm not a lawyer.

00:33:04.282 --> 00:33:05.932
- And by the way who reads the...

00:33:07.479 --> 00:33:12.120
- The disclaimer, the legal
disclaimer that when you

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:15.630
and I always thought that
when you click yes I agree,

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:18.230
that some type of really
weird things should happen.

00:33:18.230 --> 00:33:19.741
Like you hear a knock on the door

00:33:19.741 --> 00:33:22.485
and somebody's like yeah,
we're here for your wife, what?

00:33:22.485 --> 00:33:25.346
Yeah man, when you click that agree...

00:33:25.346 --> 00:33:26.533
- That's crazy stuff.

00:33:27.514 --> 00:33:28.910
So I'm not a lawyer,

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:30.670
so I'm not gonna give
you a good legal answer

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:33.067
but it's definitely true that I think

00:33:33.067 --> 00:33:36.760
one of the big issues right
now is like what protections,

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:39.880
legal protections do we have
for the way our data is used.

00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:41.090
Now over in Europe,

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:42.400
they actually have a much different view.

00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:44.240
Germany particularly 'cause
they have the Stasi, right?

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:47.690
So they have a lot of laws
and Google runs against these

00:33:47.690 --> 00:33:50.003
and hates them and lobbies against them.

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:53.650
I think we probably need
much much better protections

00:33:53.650 --> 00:33:54.483
in this country.

00:33:54.483 --> 00:33:55.980
- Can and should an employer hold

00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:57.530
your social media life against you?

00:33:57.530 --> 00:33:59.000
- I don't think they should, no.

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.100
I really don't think they
should most of the time

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:02.773
particularly at the hiring level, right?

00:34:02.773 --> 00:34:03.606
- [Neil] That's what I'm talking about.

00:34:03.606 --> 00:34:05.290
- Yeah, at the level of hiring, okay.

00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:07.120
- And then they say what have they posted

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:09.330
and what drunken pictures...

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:11.820
- I don't think that they
should be doing that stuff

00:34:11.820 --> 00:34:14.650
because that's tantamount
to, in the pre-internet era

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:18.179
say hey can we come home and
just rummage around your house?

00:34:18.179 --> 00:34:20.560
That was considered
unreasonable back then,

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:23.276
it should be similarly
considered unreasonable now.

00:34:23.276 --> 00:34:25.590
- That's why I'm a comedian today.

00:34:25.590 --> 00:34:28.133
All my stuff on social
media, I can't get a job.

00:34:29.471 --> 00:34:31.130
- So you tell jokes about it.

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:32.380
- Sadly.

00:34:32.380 --> 00:34:34.080
- We gotta take a break, but coming up,

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:37.923
more on the future of technology
when StarTalk returns.

00:34:39.650 --> 00:34:41.000
We're back on StarTalk

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:44.820
and I've got my expert
guest, Wired magazine

00:34:44.820 --> 00:34:47.120
and New York Times
columnist, Clive Thompson.

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:49.610
We're talking about the future
of technology, Chuck Nice.

00:34:49.610 --> 00:34:50.650
So we're not experts at this

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:52.650
but we have strong opinions on it,

00:34:52.650 --> 00:34:54.080
and he's got expertise so we're good.

00:34:54.080 --> 00:34:57.070
And we're featuring my
interview with Marques Brownlee.

00:34:57.070 --> 00:35:01.470
He's the 21, 22, 20 something year old

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:02.990
who's been reviewing technology

00:35:02.990 --> 00:35:05.550
for everybody on the internet,
and YouTube in particular

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:07.260
with millions of clips.

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:08.487
So I had to ask him

00:35:08.487 --> 00:35:11.340
where does he think technology is headed?

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:12.173
Let's check it out.

00:35:12.173 --> 00:35:15.710
What are you gonna be telling your kids?

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:18.457
When you say, "Back in
my day when I was 22,

00:35:18.457 --> 00:35:20.667
"look what we had to do, oh my gosh,

00:35:20.667 --> 00:35:22.000
"can I have sympathy please?

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:25.090
- Honestly I'm thinking
cars, I'm hoping cars,

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:27.531
how we drive cars today and we--

00:35:27.531 --> 00:35:28.500
- No, those will be really big gadget,

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:30.080
does that count as
something you would review?

00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:31.410
- I count that, I'm in the car tech.

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:33.970
I've done a couple of videos
about cars and that's--

00:35:33.970 --> 00:35:38.173
- So not just handheld,
anything electronic,

00:35:38.173 --> 00:35:39.780
IT digital gadget.

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:41.790
- I say anything with
an on button is game.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:44.000
Technically not every car has an on button

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:45.930
but if you look at some of
the more high-end cars today,

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:49.290
they've got electric systems
galore, plenty of high tech.

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:51.270
I feel like a lot of the stuff we have now

00:35:51.270 --> 00:35:53.020
that's inside of a car
that you have to control,

00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:54.450
like the steering wheel,

00:35:54.450 --> 00:35:55.830
even if you're in a self-driving car

00:35:55.830 --> 00:35:57.840
they still want you at the controls

00:35:57.840 --> 00:35:59.120
in case something might go wrong

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:01.290
because it's a computer and it's a system.

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:04.970
- That's funny because the
fact that you say that implies

00:36:04.970 --> 00:36:07.930
that something would go
wrong with the machine

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:09.690
that the human can correct,

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:11.630
rather than something
going wrong with a human

00:36:11.630 --> 00:36:14.090
that the machine needs
to correct. (laughs)

00:36:14.090 --> 00:36:15.710
Because last I checked,

00:36:15.710 --> 00:36:18.900
all those accidents on the
street are humans messing up.

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:22.480
- For sure, but even the
systems getting to the point

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:26.310
where everyone has a car that
is capable of driving itself.

00:36:26.310 --> 00:36:29.860
No one even needs that space
of a steering wheel in the car.

00:36:29.860 --> 00:36:30.693
You just kind of sit down,

00:36:30.693 --> 00:36:32.420
it's maybe a bench facing another bench

00:36:32.420 --> 00:36:34.600
and you just kind of ride
along to your destination.

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:36.860
I feel like that's way down the road.

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:38.690
We're at the point where
a car is sort of fusing

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:43.140
into the next generation
of vehicle at this point.

00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:45.480
- So if cars do all the
driving on the road,

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:48.529
then in principle you
can up the speed limit.

00:36:48.529 --> 00:36:49.579
- Yeah.

00:36:49.579 --> 00:36:50.870
(Neil laughs)

00:36:50.870 --> 00:36:52.420
Absolutely.
- Right?

00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:55.320
- 'Cause you're not at
risk of reaction time.

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:56.153
- Exactly.

00:36:56.153 --> 00:36:59.941
- Because electrons react much
faster than the human brain,

00:36:59.941 --> 00:37:02.240
even though there are
electrons in there somewhere

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:06.740
but wired for reacting
to traffic patterns,

00:37:06.740 --> 00:37:08.910
a car can way outperform a human mind.

00:37:08.910 --> 00:37:12.380
So I'm imagining you go
150, 200 miles an hour

00:37:12.380 --> 00:37:13.320
driving down the road.

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:15.930
- No problem and they'll
just weave seamlessly

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:17.610
within each other in a
way that would be scary

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:19.100
if a human was trying to do it,

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:20.320
but it'll just be totally normal.

00:37:20.320 --> 00:37:23.320
- That's right, because if
all the cars are going 200,

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:28.320
any high speed, then it's
a moving coordinate system

00:37:28.750 --> 00:37:31.324
so that a car can move in and out of that.

00:37:31.324 --> 00:37:32.157
- That's the other thing,

00:37:32.157 --> 00:37:33.300
all the cars are sort
of talking to each other

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:34.970
as points in the matrix--

00:37:34.970 --> 00:37:35.920
- I forgot about that.

00:37:35.920 --> 00:37:38.650
- Sort of avoid each other
because they already know,

00:37:38.650 --> 00:37:40.430
if car all the way on the right lane

00:37:40.430 --> 00:37:41.940
with a bunch of stuff in between it

00:37:41.940 --> 00:37:43.220
knows it wants to go to the left

00:37:43.220 --> 00:37:44.240
and car all the way on the left

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:45.620
knows it wants to go to the right,

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:46.880
and they can tell the cars around it

00:37:46.880 --> 00:37:49.240
I'm trying to go to the right,
I'm trying to go to the left,

00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:51.100
human couldn't do that with another car

00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:52.020
on the other side of the road.

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:53.636
- No, you can honk. (mimics honking)

00:37:53.636 --> 00:37:55.145
"Let me in." (laughs)

00:37:55.145 --> 00:37:58.300
- You can try but those machines
can work with each other

00:37:58.300 --> 00:37:59.280
much more efficiently.

00:37:59.280 --> 00:38:01.060
- I never considered that.
- Yeah.

00:38:01.060 --> 00:38:05.040
So it's a ballet on the freeway.

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:07.280
- It's a symphony, it's beautiful.

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:09.830
- That's beautiful,
that's really beautiful.

00:38:09.830 --> 00:38:11.850
Why do we get software updates?

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:15.810
Because they're bugs that
are found by the many users

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:18.540
that often the testing is
just let's put it out there

00:38:18.540 --> 00:38:20.240
and see what comes back, right?

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:24.350
So self-driving cars
where lives are at risk,

00:38:24.350 --> 00:38:26.780
what is the risk of there
being some kind of bug

00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:28.023
where it kills people?

00:38:29.310 --> 00:38:32.120
Or on top of that what's the
risk of hacking into a car

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:33.670
to create the accident in the first place?

00:38:33.670 --> 00:38:36.010
- I think the hacking might be
a bigger risk than the bugs.

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:39.660
I mean regulatorily it's
gonna be tricky for them

00:38:39.660 --> 00:38:41.120
to get those cars on the road.

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:43.440
'Cause cars are pretty tightly regulated

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:46.490
unless they can demonstrate
pretty persuasively

00:38:46.490 --> 00:38:47.330
there isn't a lot of bugs.

00:38:47.330 --> 00:38:49.150
Now hacking is a different thing,

00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:51.720
because now you've got a human person

00:38:51.720 --> 00:38:54.310
trying actively to break something, right?

00:38:54.310 --> 00:38:56.890
And that's a much more volatile risk.

00:38:56.890 --> 00:38:58.890
There's already been situations
where cars are out there

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:01.330
and Wired hired a couple of hackers

00:39:01.330 --> 00:39:02.550
to literally take over a car

00:39:02.550 --> 00:39:05.638
while it's going down the
highway and they did no problem.

00:39:05.638 --> 00:39:08.473
So I think, I've driven
in self-driving cars--

00:39:08.473 --> 00:39:10.663
- It's the diabolical division.

00:39:12.955 --> 00:39:15.623
- Exactly, yeah.

00:39:15.623 --> 00:39:17.548
I've ridden in Google's self-driving cars.

00:39:17.548 --> 00:39:18.740
It felt pretty comfortable.

00:39:18.740 --> 00:39:19.573
They're actually in a weird way,

00:39:19.573 --> 00:39:20.850
they're almost sort of cautious drivers.

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:23.290
It's like being there with
your grandmother at the wheel.

00:39:23.290 --> 00:39:25.440
Right now could get
faster as you point out

00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:29.230
but overall if I were to
compare it to the dangers

00:39:29.230 --> 00:39:31.513
of humans driving cars, I'm probably okay

00:39:31.513 --> 00:39:33.850
with the self-driving cars
if it's well regulated,

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:35.443
that's the big if, right?

00:39:35.443 --> 00:39:37.060
- So that's the future?

00:39:37.060 --> 00:39:38.440
- I think so, yeah.

00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:41.250
I mean I think it might
take longer than we think,

00:39:41.250 --> 00:39:43.480
you're getting these rosy
predictions of five years.

00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:45.000
- Yeah, I don't see five years.

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:47.700
But the fact is that they
are on the road right now,

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:50.194
they do work which I forget
that trucking company--

00:39:50.194 --> 00:39:51.027
- Otto.

00:39:51.027 --> 00:39:54.030
- Otto, so now Otto
has gone cross country,

00:39:54.030 --> 00:39:57.370
traversed the country several
times with their semi.

00:39:57.370 --> 00:40:02.030
And the only thing the human
being is doing is monitoring.

00:40:02.030 --> 00:40:04.340
They have a guy that sits
there and monitors, that's it.

00:40:04.340 --> 00:40:05.830
- And you could also, I mean frankly,

00:40:05.830 --> 00:40:07.560
the other reason I'm
kind of in favor of it

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:10.292
is it has potentially great
environmental benefits, right?

00:40:10.292 --> 00:40:11.770
Because you get way less idling,

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:13.270
you get way less jackrabbit starts.

00:40:13.270 --> 00:40:14.990
These are things that burn huge amounts--

00:40:14.990 --> 00:40:16.080
- What's a jackrabbit start?

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:19.930
- When you're at rest and
you suddenly burst off.

00:40:19.930 --> 00:40:21.820
'Cause humans want accelerate fast

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:24.218
where my car's like I'm
just gonna east forward.

00:40:24.218 --> 00:40:26.040
- Boy human testosterone.

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:28.613
Testosterone infused boy human.

00:40:28.613 --> 00:40:30.194
- Exactly, yeah.

00:40:30.194 --> 00:40:33.330
- (laughing) That's so true.

00:40:33.330 --> 00:40:35.260
- I didn't learn how
to drive till I was 25

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:37.840
and that's after the
boy human testosterone

00:40:37.840 --> 00:40:39.880
forces have dissipated.

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:40.713
- [Clive] Nicely done.

00:40:40.713 --> 00:40:42.720
- Yeah, it was very, and you get lower--

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:44.490
- All boys should not
be able to do anything

00:40:44.490 --> 00:40:47.640
until they're 25, let's be honest.

00:40:47.640 --> 00:40:50.150
We're three men sitting
here, we know the deal.

00:40:50.150 --> 00:40:52.240
If you're under 25 and you have a penis,

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:55.790
you're effing crazy, that's
all there is to it, okay?

00:40:55.790 --> 00:40:58.370
There's something deeply
wrong with you, you need help.

00:40:58.370 --> 00:40:59.878
You should talk to somebody.

00:40:59.878 --> 00:41:01.886
(Neil laughs)
Okay, I'm sorry.

00:41:01.886 --> 00:41:03.470
- Every single one.

00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:04.950
- Every one of you, okay?

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:07.280
- So what intrigues me is when
you have a self-driving car

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:09.400
and it's talking to other
cars, there are signals,

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:14.400
there's, I just learned that Tesla is,

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:16.030
talks to other Teslas.

00:41:16.030 --> 00:41:18.570
So if you come on to a bumpy road

00:41:18.570 --> 00:41:22.070
and then you make the adjustment
to change your suspension

00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:23.520
which of course you can do,

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:26.580
that information goes to the next Tesla,

00:41:26.580 --> 00:41:28.420
any other Tesla that will be on that road

00:41:28.420 --> 00:41:30.280
and it will preadjust the suspension

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:32.217
before it enters the
bumpy part of the road.

00:41:32.217 --> 00:41:34.840
- See this is where the
stuff just gets creepy, man.

00:41:34.840 --> 00:41:36.854
This is what I'm talking about, this is--

00:41:36.854 --> 00:41:38.363
- Don't want your car to know?

00:41:38.363 --> 00:41:41.154
- I don't want my car
talking to no other cars.

00:41:41.154 --> 00:41:43.400
Do not talk to any other cars.

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:45.729
How many times do I have to tell you?

00:41:45.729 --> 00:41:46.920
Don't talk to strangers, car.

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:48.290
Okay, stranger danger.

00:41:48.290 --> 00:41:49.836
I don't want that Tesla just like--

00:41:49.836 --> 00:41:51.460
- Don't you wanna know if
there's a hazard in the road?

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:53.747
- No, what I don't want is
that other Tesla saying,

00:41:53.747 --> 00:41:56.500
"You know your owner doesn't love you."

00:41:56.500 --> 00:41:58.300
- And the interesting point's gonna be,

00:41:58.300 --> 00:42:01.030
will there come a point
when the government decides

00:42:01.030 --> 00:42:03.534
that the self-driving
cars are so much safer

00:42:03.534 --> 00:42:06.010
that it's illegal for
a human to drive a car?

00:42:06.010 --> 00:42:06.843
- They could surely do that.

00:42:06.843 --> 00:42:08.440
However, I've thought about this.

00:42:10.698 --> 00:42:13.890
You cannot take a horse on the interstate

00:42:13.890 --> 00:42:15.090
even though there's a day when horses

00:42:15.090 --> 00:42:17.370
were the only things
that brought you around.

00:42:17.370 --> 00:42:20.240
So now if you like horses
you go to a stables

00:42:20.240 --> 00:42:21.317
where you can ride a--

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:23.910
- [Chuck] So we'll have race tracks.

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:25.150
- We'll have race tracks.

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:27.290
Then if you're still
a sports car collector

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:29.070
and you want to control your own car,

00:42:29.070 --> 00:42:31.870
you take it to the sports,
that you have a berth

00:42:31.870 --> 00:42:34.640
at the sports track, at the driving track

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:36.860
and that's where you drive
and you get your thrills,

00:42:36.860 --> 00:42:38.530
just like the horseback riders do.

00:42:38.530 --> 00:42:42.013
- So the Mustang will just
be, just like a real mustang.

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:47.450
You gotta go to the
ranch, go to the stables

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:49.350
and pull your Mustang out of the garage

00:42:49.350 --> 00:42:50.850
to take it for a ride.
- Giddy up.

00:42:50.850 --> 00:42:52.540
Oh man.

00:42:52.540 --> 00:42:56.180
- I asked Marques, is there
any other future of technology

00:42:56.180 --> 00:42:59.160
that he's excited about or
intrigued by, let's check it out.

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:01.560
When you're the ripe old age of 32,

00:43:01.560 --> 00:43:03.270
how do you want to be living life?

00:43:03.270 --> 00:43:06.263
What technology do you
want to be surrounding you?

00:43:07.552 --> 00:43:08.634
- Wow, 10 years from now, let's see.

00:43:08.634 --> 00:43:12.400
I hope car tech is on another level,

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:15.211
I hope that's the most exciting part.

00:43:15.211 --> 00:43:16.640
- And that's the right
horizon for this to kick in

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.850
if it's gonna do it, all right.

00:43:18.850 --> 00:43:21.920
- I hope the handheld devices we have

00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:23.220
are no longer handheld,

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:25.780
and that's pretty ambitious
for 10 years I think.

00:43:25.780 --> 00:43:28.320
But communication--
- Where are they?

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:31.245
Well someone would argue
they're like in your arm

00:43:31.245 --> 00:43:33.753
or attached, you've seen
wearable tech smartwatches

00:43:33.753 --> 00:43:36.920
that kind of shrink down
and end up on your wrist

00:43:36.920 --> 00:43:38.470
or on your ear and around on your neck

00:43:38.470 --> 00:43:39.980
or something like that.

00:43:39.980 --> 00:43:43.960
- But how about the biology
technology interface?

00:43:43.960 --> 00:43:44.793
- [Marques] Interfusion?

00:43:44.793 --> 00:43:47.160
- Yeah, put a little USB thing right here.

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:47.993
- Yeah, right in your neck--

00:43:47.993 --> 00:43:51.040
- They kind of did that
in, no one called it that

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:55.967
but there were USB ponytails in "Avatar".

00:43:55.967 --> 00:43:57.616
Remember that?

00:43:57.616 --> 00:44:00.320
Where they'd take their hair

00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:01.660
and they'd plug into the plant

00:44:01.660 --> 00:44:04.099
and they would communicate
with one another.

00:44:04.099 --> 00:44:04.932
- That's weird.

00:44:04.932 --> 00:44:07.142
- [Neil] USB ponytail,
that's what that was.

00:44:07.142 --> 00:44:07.975
- Yeah, that's the kind
of thing I see though

00:44:07.975 --> 00:44:11.840
is like a much more portable
but complete version

00:44:11.840 --> 00:44:16.340
of your digital self to just exist

00:44:16.340 --> 00:44:17.550
and be able to move around.

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:20.250
- So Clive, how real is this interface

00:44:20.250 --> 00:44:21.500
of biology and technology?

00:44:21.500 --> 00:44:24.536
And I ask because do I need my iPhone

00:44:24.536 --> 00:44:26.650
neurologically attached to me

00:44:26.650 --> 00:44:29.580
when it's sitting at my
fingertips to begin with.

00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:30.480
Is that the same thing?

00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:32.190
- It's awfully intimate right now, right?

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:33.370
It's right there.

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:34.203
- [Neil] Completely.

00:44:34.203 --> 00:44:35.470
- Yeah, they talk about
wearable technology.

00:44:35.470 --> 00:44:36.870
We already have wearable technology.

00:44:36.870 --> 00:44:39.076
We're all carrying phones with us, right?

00:44:39.076 --> 00:44:39.909
- [Neil] It's in my pocket.

00:44:39.909 --> 00:44:41.490
- But there's people working,

00:44:41.490 --> 00:44:43.850
Google's working on these contact lenses

00:44:43.850 --> 00:44:46.160
that display technology on them.

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:47.450
They're working on that quite seriously.

00:44:47.450 --> 00:44:49.040
- So any ethical frontiers here

00:44:49.040 --> 00:44:50.448
that we don't know about yet?

00:44:50.448 --> 00:44:51.980
Bioethical.

00:44:51.980 --> 00:44:55.520
- I mean the bioethical
frontiers concerns are always,

00:44:55.520 --> 00:44:57.440
do you produce something that's great

00:44:57.440 --> 00:45:00.020
for intelligence, performance and ability

00:45:00.020 --> 00:45:02.410
but is it so expensive that
only rich people get it?

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:04.220
- I heard a rebuttal to
that from Ray Kurzweil

00:45:04.220 --> 00:45:06.140
'cause we had him on StarTalk, all right?

00:45:06.140 --> 00:45:08.770
He said no that's not a problem,
the haves and the have-nots

00:45:08.770 --> 00:45:09.730
and I said why?

00:45:09.730 --> 00:45:11.750
He said because when it first comes out,

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:15.508
it never works well anyway and
it's stupendously expensive

00:45:15.508 --> 00:45:17.930
and it's not, and so yes,
only a few people have it

00:45:17.930 --> 00:45:20.323
but only when it becomes mass-market

00:45:20.323 --> 00:45:22.310
does it become truly functional.

00:45:22.310 --> 00:45:23.507
- That's true.

00:45:23.507 --> 00:45:26.360
- He's right, when phones first came out,

00:45:26.360 --> 00:45:29.227
you had to be pretty rich to get a phone.

00:45:29.227 --> 00:45:30.330
- First computers were pretty useless.

00:45:30.330 --> 00:45:31.300
- They're useless and the first phones

00:45:31.300 --> 00:45:33.920
are completely useless and
the ones that are mass-market

00:45:33.920 --> 00:45:35.090
are the ones that are most,

00:45:35.090 --> 00:45:37.130
so that's why I don't
see that as a problem.

00:45:37.130 --> 00:45:38.530
- I mean I hope he's right shall we say.

00:45:38.530 --> 00:45:39.880
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:42.611
So Clive, it's been great having you.

00:45:42.611 --> 00:45:44.110
- It's been great being on,
great conversation guys.

00:45:44.110 --> 00:45:45.240
- We gotta bring you back.

00:45:45.240 --> 00:45:46.940
We'll surely find some other excuse.

00:45:46.940 --> 00:45:48.334
- Yeah, you're much
better than the other--

00:45:48.334 --> 00:45:51.584
(Neil and Chuck laugh)

00:45:53.620 --> 00:45:54.950
- I appreciate it.

00:45:54.950 --> 00:45:57.140
- Well cool, Chuck, any
final thoughts here?

00:45:57.140 --> 00:46:00.287
- No, I just think that
this is a very scary time

00:46:00.287 --> 00:46:03.164
if you're--
- A happy thought, Chuck.

00:46:03.164 --> 00:46:04.950
Oh, I'm sorry, technology is wonderful.

00:46:04.950 --> 00:46:06.780
I'm so happy that we have it, yes

00:46:06.780 --> 00:46:08.987
and I think the human race
is so much better off now

00:46:08.987 --> 00:46:12.490
though we all have our faces
stuck in some stupid phone

00:46:12.490 --> 00:46:14.477
instead of talking to one another.

00:46:14.477 --> 00:46:17.040
- I think we are still in our infancy.

00:46:17.040 --> 00:46:20.170
Do you know it took multiple centuries

00:46:20.170 --> 00:46:22.960
after the printing press
before anyone figured out

00:46:22.960 --> 00:46:25.210
that you could make
something called a newspaper?

00:46:25.210 --> 00:46:26.090
- [Chuck] True.

00:46:26.090 --> 00:46:28.810
- So it was you had broadsheets and things

00:46:28.810 --> 00:46:32.280
but a routinely produced newspaper,

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:37.030
that took like 400 years,
300 something years.

00:46:37.030 --> 00:46:40.300
So here we are at the
dawn of the internet,

00:46:40.300 --> 00:46:42.760
even though it's 10, 15, 20 years old,

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:45.210
I think we don't yet know,

00:46:45.210 --> 00:46:46.890
we're still playing with ourselves, right?

00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:48.543
And I don't think we have,

00:46:50.246 --> 00:46:52.410
I think there's a level of maturity

00:46:52.410 --> 00:46:55.370
of how to use the power of this technology

00:46:55.370 --> 00:46:56.730
that we have yet to attain.

00:46:56.730 --> 00:46:58.600
- I just love that you
said level of maturity

00:46:58.600 --> 00:46:59.810
after you said playing with yourself

00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:02.143
and I'm sitting here like I can't help it.

00:47:03.510 --> 00:47:06.360
By the way the number one
thing that the internet causes,

00:47:07.654 --> 00:47:09.677
people playing with themselves.

00:47:09.677 --> 00:47:13.580
- So I just think there's a
level of technological maturity

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:14.980
that we have yet to achieve.

00:47:16.006 --> 00:47:18.920
And I don't know when, is it
50 years, is it 100 years,

00:47:18.920 --> 00:47:20.850
150 years, I don't know.

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:25.850
But only then can we really
say that it doesn't own us,

00:47:27.220 --> 00:47:28.343
we own it.

00:47:29.630 --> 00:47:32.900
You've been watching and
probably more likely listening

00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:35.640
to StarTalk, and I've been
your host, Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:47:35.640 --> 00:47:37.090
your personal astrophysicist.

00:47:37.090 --> 00:47:38.900
Chuck, thanks for being here.

00:47:38.900 --> 00:47:40.417
Clive, good to have you.

00:47:40.417 --> 00:47:43.170
And I publicly thank Marques Brownlee

00:47:43.170 --> 00:47:45.010
for agreeing to that interview.

00:47:45.010 --> 00:47:49.363
And until next time as always
I bid you to keep looking up.

00:47:49.363 --> 00:47:51.863
(funky music)

